    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 292324\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n425 PM PDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Chances for isolated to scattered storms linger, primarily in \n  northwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin.\n\n* Elsewhere, cooling temperatures and gusty southwest winds will be \n  the main story.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Friday.\n\nLingering moisture and upper-level support from a Pacific trough \nwill spark isolated to scattered storms this afternoon. Best odds \n(20-50%) are in the southern Great Basin (where the best forcing is) \nand northwestern AZ (where the best moisture is). Relatively quick \nstorm motions and modest moisture should limit the flash flood \nthreat (going to need training storms). However, DCAPE values of \n1000-1500 J/kg and 20-35 knots of effective wind shear will keep \nthe threat of severe storms around. Tomorrow, ensemble guidance \nhas the tail end of the trough breaking off and digging into \ncentral CA. This pushes most of the moisture into our far \neastern/southeastern zones on Sunday, keeping PoPs confined to \nthese areas at 10-25%. However, models show this feature ejecting \non Monday with notable moisture advection ahead of it. Timing of \nthe shortwave ejection (relative to peak diurnal heating) is going\nto be critical for storm chances on Monday. Currently, most \nguidance brings it through during the morning and early afternoon,\nfavoring storms in eastern Mohave and Lincoln counties. A slower \ntrack could bring these better chances farther west. Beyond \nMonday, confidence in convective potential decreases due to \nuncertainty in Karina's (currently a tropical storm) interaction \nwith the broader Pacific trough. GEFS has the storm stronger and \nfarther northeast, allowing more moisture to be stripped off by \nthe trough and advected into the I-15 corridor and points south. \nThe ECMWF ensemble has the storm weaker and farther southwest, \nresulting in less moisture advection into our area. Ultimately, \nexpecting precipitation chances on Tuesday and beyond to be lower \n(10-20%), but cannot rule out a more active day during that time \nperiod if moisture advection is on the higher end.\n\nFor places that don't see precipitation (most of the Mojave Desert), \nthe main story is going to be the cooler temperatures and increased \nwinds. The presence of the aforementioned trough and mid-week high \nclouds streaming in off of Karina will cause temperatures to \nsteadily cool over the coming days. By Wednesday, forecast highs are \n~5 degrees below normal for early September. At the same time, the \nstronger southwest flow aloft will translate to gusty southwest \nwinds at the surface. Expecting widespread gusts of 20-35 mph \nthrough the forecast period, with isolated instances of 40-45 mph \npossible (40% chance) in places like Barstow, Kingman, and Desert \nRock. In the second half of the week, temperatures look to rebound \nback towards normal thanks to subtle height rises.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...South to \nsouthwest winds (200 to 240 degrees true) with occasional gusts of \n25 to 30 knots will persist through sunset. Winds will diminish this \nevening and overnight, although occasional gusts to near 20 knots \nwill remain possible into Sunday morning. Southwest winds will \nincrease again Sunday afternoon with gusts to 25 knots likely. VFR \nconditions will prevail with cloud bases generally remaining AOA \n12kft AGL through Sunday. A few isolated thunderstorms will continue \nwell east of the terminal area into this evening, and some minor \nimpacts to the eastern approaches are still possible. Temperatures \nwill exceed 100F through 03Z this evening. Readings on Sunday will \ntop out near 101F during the late afternoon. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly winds \nwill continue across the region into this evening, with gusts of 25 \nto 30 knots possible around most terminals. Winds will diminish \novernight. The exception will be across the western Mojave Desert, \nincluding KDAG, where winds will favor a westerly direction with \ngusts of 20 to 30 knots through the late evening. Isolated areas of \nconvection will continue through sunset across the southern Great \nBasin and western Arizona, with gusty erratic winds, lightning, and \nCIGs below 10kft possible with any storms that develop. Elsewhere, \nVFR conditions will prevail with cloud bases remaining AOA 12kft \nAGL. Isolated convection will be possible again Sunday, mainly \nacross far eastern areas of the region. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
